> >We have a customer that uses OSF/1 and they have just reported a printing
> >problem.
> >
> >When a file is printed useing the lpr -P{printer} {filename}
> >
> >they get the message....
> >
> >lpr:connect:no such file or directory
> >jobs queued, but cannot start daemon
> >
> >
> >
> >Does anyone have any ideas ?
> >
> >jeff@matrix-pubs.co.uk


The message actually means that the parent lpd daemon
is not running on the host.  lpr is trying to connect to
it via a local socket to /dev/printer (which gets created
when the parent lpd is run), to tell it that it just put a
job in the queue, but fails because the device file doesn't 
exist (when lpd isn't running).  However, the job data was
put in the queue.

I'm guessing that someone figured out how to restart
the daemon, but for the record, as root do this:

	# /sbin/init.d/lpd start

The parent lpd puts its process-id in /usr/spool/lpd.lock,
so use

	# ps aux | grep /usr/lbin/lpd

to see if it is really running.  Note that if the parent
dies or is killed, it's possible to have a child lpd
running (or hung), which exists to process jobs in a
particular queue.

-mark.



