In article <3540773A.9FB66F6E@agnld.uni-potsdam.de>,
Joerg-Uwe Tessmer  <jut@agnld.uni-potsdam.de> wrote:
>Rene Akeret wrote:
>
>> Im not able to do a su to root from any users. A normal login as root is
>> possible.
>> There is always an error like this : You have no permission to do su.
>
>only you are  a member of the group 0 (/etc/group), then you have
>permissions to su to root.

Which is one of the most idiotic security features that I have ever met.
A typical Unix system may have half-a-dozen documented ways to become
root, and this restriction affects none of the others.  For example:

login (sometimes, but with some constraints)
rlogin `hostname` -l root
telnet `hostname`
xdm

I have fairly often been caught by a major upgrade overwriting /etc/group,
so su doesn't work, and have never had any difficulty in bypassing the
check.


