nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk (Nick Maclaren) writes:
> If you can lock out all the other methods then, yes, I agree that it
> could be useful.  I should be interested (seriously) to know which
> version of OSF/1 has enough control to do that.  I did actually try to
> restrict root login, and gave up when I found that I couldn't block
> most of the cases that were likely to be used by a hacker, but could
> make life more difficult for myself :-(

/etc/securettys covers most of the root login cases.  As far as I can
tell, it's been part of OSF1 "forever" (ours has a 1990 OSF copyright),
and the default on Digital Unix (checked on 3.2g and 4.0b) seems to be 

/dev/console
local:0
:0

If you can rlogin or telnet to a Digital Unix system and login as root,
then someone must have added a "ptys" entry to securettys (unless you
have "enhanced security" enabled, in which case some completely different
database applies).
