On Mon, 13 Apr 1998 17:46:40 -0400, Peter Smith <psmith01@mindspring.com>
wrote:

> Are there any pointers I can get regarding  the configuration of a tape
> drive in my alpha 433 DU  4.0 D workstation??  It's a (SN-TLZ09-LK)
> Internal 4MM DAT 4/8GB drive that is already in my system - but from
> what I can tell - that's about it.  I looked for 'tz' and 'ta' stuff in
> the /dev directory but didn't find anything.  I also have 2 hard drives,
> a floppy, and a cdrom drive - all of which I was able to find 'some'
> kind of 'device' file for in the /dev directory.
> 
> So do I begin with Makedev?  Is is simpler thru the GUI??

When I added a tape drive to one of our systems, first I needed to
reconfigure the kernel to find the new drive (which involved having the
kernel probe a new controller). You may not need to do that, but don't
be surprised if you do.

You can check to see if the kernel is finding the tape drive in
question, by looking in the binary.errlog file. See the uerf(8) man
page if you need help with reading that file.

Look in the entry for a (recent) system restart for a line indicating
that your tape drive has been found, ie:

                                  tz21 at scsi2 target 5 lun 0 (LID=6) 
                                  _(DEC     TLZ09     (C)DEC 0173) 

Then in the /dev directory, as root, type './MAKEDEV tzXX' where 'XX' is
the number that came up for the device in question. That will make the
necessary device files to communicate with the tape drive.

Upon re-reading your message, it occurs to me that you may be just
looking for the wrong file names...

look for /dev/rmt?[ahlm] and /dev/nrmt?[ahlm]

The nrmt devices are non-rewinding. Actually, you can get a more
detailed explanation than I can give you in the tz(7) man page.

I hope that helps...

